<p>You have accessed the Foundation’s Level 4 file network.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’m not sure why this is coloured in blue? Usually, blue is used only to highlight updates to an article.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Input: * * * * *</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At first glance, I wasn’t sure <em>what</em> I was reading. Maybe you can make it clearer that we’re looking at a computer terminal screen. This could be “Input Password:” for example.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I heard this particular incident stirred quite a ruckus for the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This line is severely out of place in terms of tone. Notice how the previous lines adopt a formal, clinical tone of a computer’s voice. Suddenly, this line appears with an opinion. Unless this serves a purpose later in the article, I would rephrase/cut this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>arrive at a secluded car park 20 meters away from Sally’s Catalogue of Pages</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Keep your tenses consistent. In the following sentence, you swap to past tense. “arrive” -&gt; “arrived” then.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Matthew Pickett spotted them and questioned them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remove redundant words. Here, “them” is repeated twice, in close proximity. Get rid of the first one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The two men intended to enter the store and claim it</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does “claim it” mean? That’s an odd choice of words. Did you mean “rob” it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No wallet was reported by staff</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ah. You see how it wasn’t clear the store was a lost-and-found office from the start?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>PoI-5641 distanced himself from the officers</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does this mean? If he ran, just say he ran.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The man continued to claim innocence</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Which man?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>what he claimed to be an illegal arrest, backing towards his vehicle.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My impressions of this initial log aren’t positive. It’s overly detailed and the format doesn’t suit that of a Tale. The freedom of the formatting means you’re allowed to write scenes of dialogue using regular prose. Instead, you’ve chosen to write in the style of a traditional log in an scp article. Consider how this scene and the tension would have been elevated <strong>so much more</strong> if you had written this as an actual video/audio recording.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Officer Pickett then struck the PoI repeatedly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why did this happen? Police officers don’t (usually initiate violence unless they feel their lives are being threatened.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Aftermath: PoI-5641 and PoI-5642 were detained by Springfield Police and are being held in Springfield Municipal Jail.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Notice how by the end of this first entry, <strong>I still don’t know what’s anomalous about both PoIs or the GoI they belong to.</strong> This is a serious error. You only have the first page to hook a reader’s attention, and you’ve wasted it by just portraying a regular robbery and arrest. Unless I missed something, I don’t see any anomalous effects or reasons for the Foundation to get involved.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>— Geoffrey Barraclough, Site-681 Director</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ah? So the Foundation raided and took over the base? I must have missed that beat.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The five items that were being transferred to this site</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What five items?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>though their definition of anomalous isn’t as…developed as what the Foundation handles with</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this makes me question the Foundation’s involvement at this point. I think you need to highlight the stakes / danger of the scp or GoI MUCH earlier.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>he made a post listing Site-681 as a location of interest.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This shouldn’t be sufficient grounds for investigation. What you’re suggesting is that the Foundation investigates internet conspiracy theories and raids them based on unsubstantiated info. Perhaps tell us <em>why</em> it’s a location of interest from the start? Give us anomalous sightings (whether fabricated or not).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Freyberg: Any information we find needs to be given to the investigation committee, which you already told us is being organized by the O5s.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unclear what this last section of the log is meant to convey. You’re firing off names and connections and bureaucracy quite rapidly, and it’s hard to keep up. I’d highly recommend trimming this portion, especially if it isn’t relevant to the story.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Input: Investigation Committee Operation Log-06</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This entire log is unnecessary as it’s repeated information from the previous one. You’ve already mentioned the PoI to be captured. Keep either one but not both.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Because they were investigating 2230&#160;9th Street. The 2330 on the post was a typo.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’m really torn on this. I know I was one of the reviewers who gave you the initial greenlight, but perhaps I should have asked you to clarify the format in which you’d be writing in. A lot of the initial beats and tension you were aiming for (mass conspiracy, the head researcher’s ambition for a promotion, a possible mole in the Foundation) did not come through during this Tale. Instead, it read mostly like a series of logs in every other scp article, just that in this case, it wasn’t clear <em>what</em> the anomaly was, why it was dangerous to begin with, and basically, what the whole fuss was about.</p>
<p>There seems to be <em>some</em> goal of the PoI to uncover secrecy and promote transparency, but it’s muddled throughout the logs, and only mentioned in one or two lines throughout. A lot of your detail could be cut, for example, and the bare-bones story would still be there.</p>
<p>Personally, I would no-vote this in its current state. I feel you need to make the anomaly much clearer at the beginning, as well as highlight the intentions and goals of the main character (which I’d advised you before). Right now, there are too many people running around, the O5 council getting involved for no reason, and the GoI coming across as trolls on the forum. I would suggest cutting characters that serve little purpose, and to reemphasise what their goals are from the beginning – for example, do you really need:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Frederic Pittman, O5 Council Representative, Kendall Callaghan, Ethics Committee Representative, Conan Freyberg, Head of Investigation Committee-9182 and Alexander Bradford, Head of Committee Operations</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Lastly, do consider revamping this to a proper Tale format, which means majority of the article should be written in regular prose. There still could be logs here and there, but don’t forget: a Tale should be a short story. I should be able to hear your voice as the author throughout. At least, that’s my definition of it. Of course, feel free to look for other reviewers to get second opinions.</p>
<p>If you decide to revise the draft, do drop me another PM and I’ll do my best to respond again. All the best with the writing for now!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

